<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- Bridges P2P channels to the container's loopback proxies, and (eventually) accepts
         inbound WebRTC ICE hole-punch traffic — see design spec §2 "no standing listening ports"
         (ICE binds ephemeral ports; that's not a stable inbound service). -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- Own bookmark store for non-iCloud sites (design spec §2 "File access") — the main app's
         bookmarks are app-scoped and don't transfer to this bundle ID. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- Apple Containerization framework (#69): boot local OCI containers, same as Anglesite.app. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.virtualization</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- NO com.apple.developer.icloud-container-identifiers and NO
         com.apple.security.application-groups here yet — both require a provisioning profile
         even under ad-hoc/Manual signing (see Resources/Anglesite-Debug.entitlements' identical
         note re: #1038). Task 6 Step 5 documents the manual Apple Developer portal step that
         unblocks adding them; until then this file matches the CI-safe, no-Team-required shape
         every other Debug entitlements file in this repo keeps.

         STEPS still outstanding for #1208 P2. Steps 1 and 3 are Apple Developer portal steps
         (Team KH7H8Y25RT) that cannot be performed from this repo — each capability has to be
         enabled on the App ID in the portal first, and only then added here. Step 2 is NOT a
         portal step (see its own note); it is a project-configuration decision.

         1. CloudKit signaling. Add to THIS file:
              com.apple.developer.icloud-container-identifiers = [iCloud.io.dwk.anglesite]
              com.apple.developer.icloud-services            = [CloudKit]
            and add "CloudKit" to the existing com.apple.developer.icloud-services array in
            Resources/Anglesite.entitlements, which today lists only CloudDocuments (enough for
            iCloud Drive site storage, not for CloudKit).
            Until that lands, the helper's `connect` invocation degrades to a local directory
            mailbox for the signaling transport. It does NOT construct any CloudKit type: this
            process checks its own code signature first, because CKContainer(identifier:) traps
            the process (SIGTRAP) rather than failing when the entitlement is absent. See
            `processCarriesCloudKitEntitlement(containerIdentifier:)` in
            Sources/anglesite-remote-helper/main.swift. Signing and key pinning stay live in the
            degraded mode, so only cross-network rendezvous is lost.

         2. Shared keychain access group. The helper signs signaling payloads with a
            DevicePairingKeyPair read from the Keychain, but Keychain items are scoped per bundle
            ID, so this helper cannot read the key the main app's Settings pane generated and
            published in its pairing QR code, and mints its own instead. A shared
            keychain-access-group entitlement on both bundle IDs is what makes the two the same
            device identity.

            CORRECTION (2026-08-14): this step used to open with an Apple Developer portal
            sub-step, on the assumption that Keychain Sharing needed a portal capability the way
            CloudKit and App Groups do. It does not — every App ID carries Keychain Sharing
            implicitly. But removing that false blocker uncovered a real one, measured rather than
            assumed: adding

                <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
                <array><string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)io.dwk.anglesite.shared</string></array>

            to THIS file fails the default Debug build outright with

                error: "AnglesiteRemote" requires a provisioning profile. Enable development
                signing and select a provisioning profile in the Signing & Capabilities editor.

            Removing those four lines makes the same build succeed; substituting a literal
            "M34HBJZNYA.io.dwk.anglesite.shared" for the $(AppIdentifierPrefix) form fails
            identically, so it is the entitlement itself and not the variable expansion. So the
            portal half of the assumption was wrong but the signing half was not: as far as
            codesign is concerned keychain-access-groups sits with the iCloud and Associated
            Domains entitlements the top of this block already describes, needing a real
            provisioning profile that ad-hoc/no-Team signing cannot self-grant. It differs from
            them only in that no portal capability has to be enabled first.

            That is a blocker here specifically because THIS file has no Debug counterpart. The
            Anglesite target splits into Resources/Anglesite.entitlements (Release) and
            $(ANGLESITE_DEBUG_ENTITLEMENTS) (Debug, defaulting to the CI-safe
            Resources/Anglesite-Debug.entitlements); AnglesiteMobile splits the same way. The
            AnglesiteRemote target in project.yml sets one CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS for both
            configs, so anything added here lands in the Debug build too and breaks the
            no-Apple-account clone-and-build promise in README.md / CONTRIBUTING.md, along with
            CI's Debug lane.

            Unblocking it is an owner decision about which configurations get the capability, not
            a mechanical edit. The repo-idiomatic shape would be to mirror the split the other two
            targets already use: add Resources/AnglesiteRemote-Debug.entitlements (no keychain
            group), add an ANGLESITE_REMOTE_DEBUG_ENTITLEMENTS indirection to
            xcconfig/Signing-Debug.xcconfig, point AnglesiteRemote's Debug config at it in
            project.yml, and put the group in this (Release) file plus
            Resources/Anglesite.entitlements. Note what that does and does not buy: sharing would
            then be live only in Release/real-Team builds. To exercise it in a local Debug run the
            owner would also need Team-carrying opt-in Debug variants for BOTH targets, in the
            style of the existing Resources/Anglesite-Debug-iCloud.entitlements.

            The code side stays prepared and inert in the meantime: KeychainStore has an optional
            `accessGroup:` init parameter defaulting to nil (today's behavior), plus the shared
            group string as KeychainStore.sharedPairingAccessGroup — whose team prefix was
            corrected to M34HBJZNYA (the team whose Apple Development certificate signs local
            builds) from an earlier assumed KH7H8Y25RT; KeychainStoreTests pins its exact shape.
            $(AppIdentifierPrefix) is a build-time substitution that nothing expands at runtime,
            which is why that Swift constant carries the team prefix literally and must be kept in
            step with whatever team ends up signing.

            When the configuration question above is settled, what remains is mechanical:
              a. Add the key/array to this file and to Resources/Anglesite.entitlements (or to
                 whichever per-config files that decision selects). The two strings must match
                 exactly; a mismatch is silent — each process keeps its own item and pairing
                 simply never verifies — which is why the runtime string lives in one Swift
                 constant rather than being written out at each call site.
              b. Confirm KeychainStore.sharedPairingAccessGroup still equals the expansion of the
                 string in (a) for the team that actually signs the build.
              c. Swap `KeychainStore()` for
                 `KeychainStore(accessGroup: KeychainStore.sharedPairingAccessGroup)` at both
                 pairing-key call sites, which must move together (one alone only relocates the
                 mismatch):
                   - Sources/AnglesiteApp/DevicePairingSettingsView.swift, in generateQRCode()
                   - Sources/anglesite-remote-helper/main.swift, in helperSigningKey()
                 Sources/AnglesiteCore/Platform/SecretStore.swift's
                 readDevicePairingKeyPair()/writeDevicePairingKeyPair() need NO change — verified
                 store-agnostic; they carry a pointer to this checklist only.
              d. Verify on a real signed build (the only place this is observable): launch the
                 app, generate the QR in Settings ▸ Devices, then confirm the helper logs no
                 "generated this helper's device pairing key" line on a subsequent cold start —
                 meaning it found the app's existing key rather than minting its own. Delete both
                 processes' pre-existing per-bundle keys first, or the stale ones mask the result.
              e. Drop the now-stale "not the same key today" paragraph on helperSigningKey() in
                 Sources/anglesite-remote-helper/main.swift, and this note. helperDeviceID()'s
                 paragraph stays: that gap is a UserDefaults suite mismatch, a different mechanism
                 that this step does not touch, and it needs the App Group from the note above.

         3. Ubiquity container. RemoteSiteResolver's zero-prompt iCloud path stays dormant until
            this bundle ID carries the iCloud container entitlement from step 1; every site
            resolution takes the one-time access-panel/bookmark path in the meantime. That is
            already documented on RemoteSiteResolver itself. -->
</dict>
</plist>
